"About British Nordic Walking (https://britishnordicwalking.org.uk/pages/who-we-are)\nciteturn97search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \n\n## About British Nordic Walking\n\nImage\n\nBritish Nordic Walking is the UK’s only representative of the International Nordic Walking Federation (INWA) and the only body to offer training in the internationally recognised INWA 10 Step Method of Nordic Walking. As such, our instructor training is always based on the latest research from around the world with regard to the technique itself and its health and fitness benefits.\n\nBut more than that we are run by the most enthusiastic Nordic Walkers. Everyone from our CEO and our National Trainers, to our Instructors and our growing community of Nordic Walkers is passionate about what we do.\n\nAs a Community Interest Company, community is at the heart of everything we do. Our profits are reinvested in initiatives to further develop Nordic Walking as a popular activity for health, well-being and fitness in the UK.\n\n## Our Community Interest Statement\n\nOur mission is focused on four principles:\n\n  * Training individuals from local authorities, the health sector, charities, community groups and the private sector to become instructors and walk leaders, with training to international standards approved by INWA, Nordic Walking’s international body.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJob Instructor Training (https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-PR32_5200-7e31bf44f47cb2d10cfb1c0b32401006/html/GOVPUB-PR32_5200-7e31bf44f47cb2d10cfb1c0b32401006.htm)\nciteturn97search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 84.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; \n    -\tStress that the successful instruction followed a definite but simple plan.\n    6.\tEstablish'the FOUR BASIC STEPS.\n    -\tDraw out by brief discussion what the instructor did in each step.\n    NOTE: To the extent you can, cite examples from your own experience to illustrate some of the points as you put them on the board.\n    —\tList the STEPS and sub-points on the board. Follow the \"How to Instruct\" card. Get every point on the board.\n    -\tStress: IF THE WORKER HASN'T LEARNED, THE INSTRUCTOR HASN’T TAUGHT.\n    NOTE: Following are the FOUR STEPS with slightly abbreviated wording to be placed on the board if space is limited.\n    STEP I. PREPARE the worker.\n    Put at ease. Correct position.\n    References\n    I\n    WORK FROM THIS OUTLINE—DON'T TRUST TO MEMORY\n    Sessions Outline\n    11\n    Time\n    Table\n    1\thr.\n    55 min.\n    to here\n    (Allow\n    5 min.)\n    2\thrs.\n    to here\n    Find out what he already knows. References Get him interested.\n    (Cite examples, if you can, from your own experience.)\n    STEP II. PRESENT the operation.\n    Tell, show, illustrate, ask.\n    One step at a time, clearly, patiently.\n    Stress key points.\n    •\tNo more than he can master.\n    (Cite examples, if you can, from your own experience.)\n    STEP III. TRY OUT his performance.\n    Have him do job. Tell and show\n    YOU.\n    Check key points.\n    Question.\n    Correct errors. REPEAT.\n    (Cite examples, if you can, from your own experience.)\n    STEP IV. FOLLOW UP.\n    Put on his own.\n    Who helps him—no one else.\n    Check frequently.\n    Taper off extra coaching.\n    (Cite examples, if you can, from your own experience.)\n    IV.\tCONCLUDING THE SESSION.\n    Pages\n    1.\tDistribute \"How to Instruct\" cards.\t55-56\n    2.\tExplain that the following sessions will be devoted to \"learning by doing\" in which each member will be given opportunity for practice in actual instruction.\n    3.\tCall for two volunteers to put on instructing demonstrations at the next session.\n    - Ask these two volunteers to remain after the session for further directions .\n    4.\tClose the session promptly at scheduled time with appropriate remarks.\n    WORK FROM THIS OUTLINE—DON’T TRUST TO MEMORY\n    —\tSteps omitted or not made clear.\n    -\t\"Key points\" not explained.\n    —\t\"Hazy\" or \"superficial\" explanation.\n    In Step III:\n    —\tLack of complete \"check.\"\n    -\tLack of thoroughness.\n    WORK FROM THIS OUTLINE—DON'T TRUST TO MEMORY\n    15\n    Time\n    Table\n    I\n    25 min.\n    to here\n    (Allow\n    20 min.)\n    45 min. to here\n    (Allow\n    5 min.)\n    4\n    50 min. to here\n    —\tAsk learner about specific \"key References points\" to see if instructor had taught them.\n    in step IV:\n    -\t(Don’t try to do much at this stage.)\n    4.\tThank members for their help and cooperation. Mention again that this \"volunteer job\" is just to demonstrate the need for careful preparation BEFORE instructing.\n    5.\tCall on the SECOND member who had volunteered at the first session to put on an instructing demonstration.\n    NOTE: Write steps and key points on a breakdown sheet as the demonstration is put on.\n    — After this second demonstration, bring out points as in the first demonstration.\n    6.\t    b.\tHave each member state the job he intends References to use as his practice demonstration.\n    -\tIf job selected is too big a job for simple demonstration, help member to select a simpler job.\n    -\tEach member should plan to use a job that will consume no more than a 15-minute period.\n    -\tEach should bring all necessary tools and supplies to provide for ample \"practice” in Step III.\n    c.\tBe sure each member understands that the complete, correct instruction procedure is now expected, i.e.:\n    -\tThe 4 \"get ready\" items, particularly the job breakdown. Make clear: Each member should bring his COMPLETED breakdown sheet.\n    -\tThe \"four steps\" used in the instruction itself.\n    3.\tClose meeting with BRIEF \"SALES\" STATEMENT on personal advantages of becoming good instructors.\n    a.\tThe following practice and drill sessions will enable them to \"get the feel of the instructing tools.\"\n    b.\tPractice will