Pages\n    50-52\n    —\tHave members watch their ”How to Instruct\" cards as the demonstrations are put on.\n    -\tHave them write down the key points of each demonstration on breakdown sheets.\n    -\tGradually call on members for criticisms, for example:\n    -\tOne member, the GET READY points.\n    -\tAnother, STEP I.\n    -\tAnother, STEP II.\n    -\tAnother, STEP III.\n    How to Comment on the Two Volunteer Instruction Demonstrations\n    •\n    Because this part of the session is so important the reference material is worked out in detail. You can follow it as the outline, until the volunteer demonstrations are completed.\n    I.\tKEEP CLEARLY IN MIND WHAT YOU ARE LOOKING FOR:\n    - You are seeking to have members PROVE TO THEMSELVES that instruction on the job can only be properly done when instructors GET READY to instruct properly. The sole purpose of these two demonstrations is to SELL THE NEED AND NECESSITY that Supervisors do some planning and analyzing BEFORE instructing.\n    II.\tLOOK FOR these points, in particular:\n    -\tSTOP each of the demonstrations at the end of 15 minutes, in any event. This provides a useful point for comment.\n    -\tIn Step I:\n    -\tWas the \"lead in\" natural?\n    -\tWas the instructor’s attitude toward the man constructive?\n    NOTE: Don’t spend much time on Step I at this stage of the program. It isn’t the point you are trying to emphasize. Mention it, of course, but don't try to dig into it.\n    - In Step II:\n    LOOK FOR COMPLETENESS, CLEARNESS, and ORDERLY PRESENTATION\n    -\tNote each step', as nearly as you can get it, on a Job Breakdown Sheet as the demonstration is put on.\n    -\tLeave blank spaces where the instruction is \"hazy,\" or something appears to be omitted.\n    -\tNote whatever points that appear to be \"Key Points.\" Keep on doing this even if it takes 5 or 6 Breakdown Sheets.\n    -\tNote places where the instructor \"back tracked,\" or brought in new idea's at the \"wrong place,\" or \"jumped about\" from one point to another.\n    NOTE.—STEP II is THE most important step to scrutinize for the purpose you are seeking to establish.\n    Reference Material\n    41\n    -\tIn Step III:\n    LOOK FOR COMPLETENESS OF CHECK, and HOW NEARLY THE POINTS CHECKED FOLLOWED THE POINTS PRESENTED.\n    -\tLook for new ideas presented in Step III that were omitted in Step II.\n    -\tLook particularly for the \"key” points that were missed, or not stressed, or not checked clearly.\n    — Have worker do the job.\n    — Correct errors at once.\n    - Have worker do j ob again TELLING instructor what he was doing— and why.\n    - Have worker DO and EXPLAIN what he was doing again and again until the worker knew it PERFECTLY.\n    - Ask questions that couldn’t be answered by \"yes\" or \"no\".\n    — Have worker do and explain key points. Did they stand out sharply?\n    - Did he \"grab\" tool or material out of worker’s hand to correct error or reinstruct?\n    — Was instructor’s attitude exacting yet friendly? Patient?\n    — Make a smooth transition to Step IV.\n    NOTE.—Turn to learner. Ask him \"key points\" about the job. See if he knows them. If he doesn't, stress: \"If the worker hasn't learned, the instructor hasn't taught.\"\n    NOTE.—You must maintain a searching and penetrating frame of mind toward these demonstrations. Look for the \"fine\" points where the learner might go wrong. You don't need to know the operations. Just ask searching questions. Remember, you must make instructors be more thorough, exact, and careful.\n    STEP IV:\n    - Was someone designated to whom the worker goes for help?\n    NOTE.—This is about all of Step IV that can be demonstrated in the conference room. Sometimes an instructor will walk away from the worker for a moment, letting this act indicate a passage of time in the shop. Then he returns to \"check\" on the worker's progress. This is a good practice to develop unless it will overcrowd the session time. It is even helpful to have the instructor state about how much time has elapsed. If session time is short, however, it .is well to have only the one point (mentioned above) in Step IV.\n    - Ask member, however, \"How would you 'follow-up' this man during the next several hours, and several days?\"\n    Three Special Instruction Problems\n    1.\tTHE LONG OPERATION\n    -\tHow to use the 4-step basic instructing process on a working operation that takes perhaps 3 hours or 3 days.\n    -\tThis is where correct instructing is MOST important. This is a situation where the learner and the instructor become most \"confused\" and \"lost.\"\n    -\tThe answer is to \"break up\" the long operation into small \"doses\" then put over one dose at a time.\n    A.\tExamples in the meeting room.\n    - Frequently jobs selected are: How to read the Micrometer; or Slide rule; or Vernier. When selected they should be so handled by the Trainer to illustrate the need for small unit breakdowns. These jobs should be put over by small units.\n    - Example—Micrometer\n    one unit—with 4 complete steps; parts and care of the instrument.\n    one unit—with 4 complete steps; the scales and their relation to each other.\n    one unit—with 4 complete steps; simple measurements.\n    one unit—with 4 complete steps; difficult measurements, i. e., those requiring reading in fractions of thousandths.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Related Information (https://www.mhlw.go.jp/kaiseiryokangyohou/download/pamphlet_dl_3_en_A3.pdf)\nciteturn97search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: 1.6 years ago; \n# Related Information\n\n## Programs provided by the organizations concerned\nTraining tool main text p8–9\n\n<table id=\"e1\">\n\n| Type | Training name | Content | Contact |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| General training and assistance dog users | Training for disability understanding/hospitality | The training content is built on a custom-made basis, with instructors concerned as the core of the training. Workshops are conducted upon request.<br>(1) Training for disability understanding, (2) Practical training for hospitality, and (3) 2030SDGs workshop and disability understanding training<br>*Expenses: To be discussed depending on the location and scale of the training (coordination fee, instructor fees, transportation fees, etc.)<br>*It is also possible to introduce training providers and user groups around the country close to the venue. | Japan Assistance Dog Information Center<br>Phone: 045-275-7770<br>https://www.jsdrc.jp |\n| General training and instructor training | Dispatch of instructors, on-site lectures, and training for leaders concerned regarding barrier-free access for the disabled | A program to learn the concept of the social model of disability with disabled instructors concerned and to link it to actions. \n### Steps\n**Step 1**　First, check your facility’s efforts and consider any issues  \n**Step 2**　Planning and drafting of training programs unique to your facility  \nThe organizations concerned offer various training programs, so be sure to make use of them.  \nIt is also effective to participate in training sessions held by the organizations.  \n**Step 3**　Further deepening and development of efforts at your facility  \nDeepen your learning about:  \n(1) Serving guests in different situations  \n(2) Responding to emergencies and disasters and preparing for them  \n(3) Communication and other topics according to the type of disability\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nAt lower right, a link prompt reads “▼Click here for the main text (detailed version) of the training tool.” Beneath it is a blue Japanese-language training-tool cover showing people with disabilities and companions, a QR code, and the URL https://www.mhlw.go.jp/kaiseiryoukankyohou/. To the right is an illustration of a blind person wearing a cap and dark glasses, holding a white cane, being guided by a suited attendant. Logos at top right: 厚生労働省 and 国土交通省 観光庁. Page numbers: 4 (left) and 1 (right).\n</visual_element>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbout INWA | the International Nordic Walking Federation – British Nordic Walking (https://britishnordicwalking.org.uk/pages/about-inwa)\nciteturn97search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; \n\nWhen you train with British Nordic Walking, you become part of something much bigger than a single training provider. You are joining an international network of instructors, trainers and organisations committed to delivering high quality Nordic Walking education and experiences.\n\nOur close relationship with INWA ensures our courses stay aligned with the latest international developments in technique, coaching, research and education. It also means our instructors benefit from recognition that extends beyond the UK.\n\n## INWA’s Key Areas of Work\n\n#### Education – Teaching Nordic Walking\n\nEducation has always been at the heart of INWA’s mission. INWA develops innovative teaching methods and training programmes for Nordic Walking in collaboration with experts in sport, health and fitness.\n\nUsing the internationally recognised INWA 10 Step Method, INWA has certified Nordic Walking National Trainers and Instructors in more than 40 countries worldwide. This shared educational framework helps maintain consistency, quality and professionalism across the global Nordic Walking community.\n"
